{"name":"Flier Lie","hex":"#cdb891","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"flier","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["comparative form of fly: more fly"]},{"part":"Noun","definitions":["Alternative form of flyer (more common in US, except in the sense of \"leaflet\")"]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/flier"},{"term":"lie","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Symbol","definitions":["ISO 639-3 language code for Likila."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["To rest in a horizontal position on a surface.","To be placed or situated."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/lie"}],"entries":[{"title":"Lie","blocks":[{"text":"A lie is an assertion that is believed to be false, typically used with the intention of deceiving or misleading someone. The practice of communicating lies is called lying. A person who communicates a lie may be termed a liar. Lies can be interpreted as deliberately false statements or misleading statements, though not all statements that are literally false are considered lies – metaphors, hyperboles, and other figurative rhetoric are not intended to mislead, while lies are explicitly meant for literal interpretation by their audience. Lies may also serve a variety of instrumental, interpersonal, or psychological functions for the individuals who use them."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/65/Pinocchio.jpg/330px-Pinocchio.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Lie","wikidata":"Q4925193"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/28/%22An_ostrich_only_thinks_he_%22covers_up.%22_-_NARA_-_513846.jpg/500px-%22An_ostrich_only_thinks_he_%22covers_up.%22_-_NARA_-_513846.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/28/%22An_ostrich_only_thinks_he_%22covers_up.%22_-_NARA_-_513846.jpg/1280px-%22An